{"review_id":"00001788454764715784-0001485283-000001-cycle-review-1","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:903a2a3087d19e01706f439d1f1015dd85e70617f408f791a335f9d10f5ed962","findings":[{"finding_id":"f1-poll-aborts-all-rows","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"One row's credential or client failure aborts the entire batch poll, unbounding every other PRD's wait","claim":"In `poll_once`, the per-row `AnthropicHttpClient::new` and `EnvCredentialResolver.resolve` failure arms `return` out of the whole function instead of `continue`ing to the next row, unlike the immediately preceding `batch_auth` arm which correctly `continue`s. Because the same failure recurs on every 30s tick, a single unresolvable row permanently starves every other pending batch of polling — including the deadline check that implements the configured maximum batch wait (acceptance criterion 5).","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,919 @@\n+        let api_key = match EnvCredentialResolver.resolve(&auth) {\n+            Ok(key) => key,\n+            Err(error) => {\n+                tracing::warn!(error = %error, \"batch review poll: credential resolution failed\");\n+                return;\n+            }\n+        };"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:b15ef876bf8ec2c0d0a9c37b75714988e1cd8d9e58c99a59c85c84cdd40f3e56","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73382,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:87f2f14724a3552a6698c637c4a31b2e990ae7bfabe67b4c58ca28666d9bcbce","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-b15ef876bf8ec2c0d0a9c37b75714988e1cd8d9e58c99a59c85c84cdd40f3e56-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Change both the client-construction and credential-resolution failure arms inside the `for row in pending` loop from `return` to `continue`, matching the `batch_auth` arm, so one repository's misconfiguration cannot stall polling or deadline expiry for unrelated repositories' batches.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f2-completed-rows-never-repolled","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"A batch marked `completed` is dropped from the poll set, so a failed `resume_now` strands the result","claim":"`BatchReviewRepository::submitted()` selects only `WHERE state='submitted'`, and `poll_once` iterates exactly that set. Once `apply_batch_result` transitions a row to `completed`, the only thing that consumes the result is the fire-and-forget `resume_now` task, whose failures are logged and discarded. The doc comment's claim that \"the next poll (or an operator-triggered resume) tries again from the same durable state\" is false for the poll half: the next poll can never see the row again. No accounting observation is written either, since `record_accounting` runs only inside `consume_completed`.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-storage/src/repos/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,412 @@\n+    pub fn submitted(&self) -> familiar_ai_core::Result<Vec<BatchReviewRow>> {\n+        let mut statement = self\n+            .conn\n+            .prepare(&format!(\n+                \"{SELECT_ROW} WHERE state='submitted' ORDER BY submitted_at\"\n+            ))\n+            .map_err(db)?;"},{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,919 @@\n+/// Re-drives review disposition for a PRD whose batch just resolved\n+/// (completed or fell back), reusing the ordinary implemented-checkpoint\n+/// resume path rather than a second implementation of disposition.\n+/// Failures are logged, not fatal: the next poll (or an operator-triggered\n+/// resume) tries again from the same durable state."},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:b15ef876bf8ec2c0d0a9c37b75714988e1cd8d9e58c99a59c85c84cdd40f3e56","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73382,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:87f2f14724a3552a6698c637c4a31b2e990ae7bfabe67b4c58ca28666d9bcbce","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-b15ef876bf8ec2c0d0a9c37b75714988e1cd8d9e58c99a59c85c84cdd40f3e56-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"failure_scenario":"A batch resolves; `apply_batch_result` writes `state='completed'`. The spawned `resume_now` then fails — `Database::open` errors under contention with the daemon's own connection, or `CheckpointRepository::get` returns `Ok(None)`, or `resolved_agent_entries` errors — and only logs a warning. On the next tick `submitted()` no longer returns the row, so the poller never retries; the completed reviewer output is never parsed, no `usage_observations` row is ever appended for that batch, and the PRD remains parked past its `deadline_at` because the expiry path is also gated on `state='submitted'`.","remediation":"Include `completed` rows in the poller's work set (e.g. a `pending()` query covering `state IN ('submitted','completed')`, dispatching completed rows straight to the re-drive path), so a failed `resume_now` is retried from durable state on a later tick rather than depending on an unrelated resume.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f3-assumed-rate-not-provider-cost","category":"unverifiable_claim","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Batch cost is a hardcoded half-rate assumption recorded in a `provider_cost` column, not a provider-reported cost","claim":"Acceptance criterion 4 requires the accounting observation to record \"the provider-reported cost with provider provenance, so PRD-051 comparisons measure the actual discount ... rather than assuming a rate\". The implementation records exactly an assumed rate: `BATCH_PRICE_DISCOUNT_DENOMINATOR: u64 = 2` divides the configured interactive estimate, the `UsageObservation` is written with `provider_cost_lexical: None`, and the derived estimate is then stored in the durable column `batch_reviews.provider_cost_lexical` — a name that implies a provider-reported figure. A PRD-051 comparison of batch vs interactive for the same risk class will therefore always report exactly 50%, which is the input assumption rather than a measurement, and the durable column can be conflated with real provider billing during reconciliation. Nothing in the package establishes that no provider-reported cost is obtainable (e.g. via the billing/usage surfaces the repository already models with `BillingRepository`/`ProviderCostRow`).","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,919 @@\n+const BATCH_PRICE_DISCOUNT_DENOMINATOR: u64 = 2;"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:b15ef876bf8ec2c0d0a9c37b75714988e1cd8d9e58c99a59c85c84cdd40f3e56","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73382,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:87f2f14724a3552a6698c637c4a31b2e990ae7bfabe67b4c58ca28666d9bcbce","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-b15ef876bf8ec2c0d0a9c37b75714988e1cd8d9e58c99a59c85c84cdd40f3e56-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"failure_scenario":"An operator enables batch tiering and asks PRD-051 whether the discount is real. Every batch observation's cost is `interactive_estimate / 2` by construction and the observation carries no provider cost, so the report returns a 50% saving regardless of what the provider actually billed — the assumption is unfalsifiable from the ledger. Separately, a reconciliation job reading `batch_reviews.provider_cost_lexical` treats a locally computed estimate as a provider-reported amount.","remediation":"Either record a genuine provider-reported cost with provider provenance for batch executions (reconciling against the provider billing surface, as `BillingRepository` already does), or rename the durable column and the observation provenance so the assumed-rate estimate can never be read as a provider fact, and state in the accounting record that the discount is assumed rather than measured.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f4-poller-untested","category":"test_gap","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"The daemon-owned batch poller — the mechanism acceptance criterion 3 rests on — has no test coverage","claim":"Acceptance criterion 3 requires the daemon to own batch lifecycle across restarts. That behaviour lives entirely in `run`, `poll_once`, `apply_batch_result`, and `resume_now`, none of which is exercised by any added test: `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/batch_review.rs` imports only `build_batch_reviewer`, `BatchReviewAgent`, and `BatchReviewContext`, and every test drives `BatchReviewAgent::review` directly. The restart-resume claim is thus demonstrated only for the in-slot agent path, not for the poller that is supposed to make progress when no review cycle is running — which is also why defects f1 and f2 pass all checks.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,941 @@\n+use familiar_ai_daemon::batch_review::{\n+    build_batch_reviewer, BatchReviewAgent, BatchReviewContext,\n+};"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:b15ef876bf8ec2c0d0a9c37b75714988e1cd8d9e58c99a59c85c84cdd40f3e56","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73382,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:87f2f14724a3552a6698c637c4a31b2e990ae7bfabe67b4c58ca28666d9bcbce","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-b15ef876bf8ec2c0d0a9c37b75714988e1cd8d9e58c99a59c85c84cdd40f3e56-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"failure_scenario":"A regression that makes `poll_once` skip or abort rows (as it does today for a single credential failure) or that leaves completed rows unconsumed passes `format`, `lint`, and `tests-green-crates` unchanged, because no test ever calls `poll_once`. The bounded-wait guarantee and the crash-resume guarantee are therefore unverified by the suite.","remediation":"Add integration coverage for `poll_once` against `wiremock` and an in-memory database: multiple pending rows across repositories (one with unresolvable credentials) proving later rows are still polled; a row past `deadline_at` proving expiry fallback is written without a review cycle running; and a completed-but-unapplied row proving it is retried after a failed re-drive.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f5-repository-key-identity-unverified","category":"unverifiable_claim","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"The claim that the CLI-written `repositories.<key>` matches the daemon's runtime repository key is unverified and the fixtures disagree","claim":"`resolved_tier_policy`'s doc asserts `repository_key` \"is exactly the map key the CLI writes under `repositories.<key>`\". The two producers are different code paths: the CLI derives the key from `repository_identity(&current_dir)` (canonicalized absolute worktree path — its own test fixture builds a real directory and canonicalizes it, with no `.git` component), while `run.rs` passes `slash(&context.repository.repository)`. Nothing in the change tests that these two strings are equal, and the daemon test fixtures use a third shape, the literal `\"repo/.git\"`. If they differ, `familiar-ai batch-review enable` writes a repository-scoped policy that `resolved_tier_policy`/`build_batch_reviewer` never resolve, and batch tiering silently stays off for the repository the operator just audited-enabled (acceptance criterion 6's per-repository enablement), while `poll_once`'s `batch_auth(config, &row.repository_key)` lookup would likewise miss.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/run.rs","hunk":"@@ -2437,6 +2456,16 @@ fn run_review(input: ReviewRunInput<'_>) -> Result<ReviewCycle, RunError> {\n             path_prefixes: check.path_prefixes.clone(),\n         })\n         .collect();\n+    let batch_reviewer = crate::batch_review::build_batch_reviewer(\n+        config,\n+        db.conn(),\n+        format!(\"{execution_id}-cycle\"),\n+        slash(&context.repository.repository),\n+        prd_id.into(),\n+        execution_id.into(),\n+        declared_risk_classes.clone(),\n+        &reviewer_adapter,\n+    );\n     let coordinator = ReviewCoordinator {"},{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/cli/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,372 @@\n+    /// `Config::validate` requires every `repositories` key to be an\n+    /// absolute, canonicalizable worktree path, so the fixture repository\n+    /// must be a real directory rather than an arbitrary string.\n+    fn repository_key(root: &std::path::Path) -> String {\n+        let repository = root.join(\"repo\");\n+        std::fs::create_dir_all(&repository).unwrap();\n+        repository\n+            .canonicalize()\n+            .unwrap()\n+            .to_string_lossy()\n+            .into_owned()\n+    }"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:b15ef876bf8ec2c0d0a9c37b75714988e1cd8d9e58c99a59c85c84cdd40f3e56","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73382,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:87f2f14724a3552a6698c637c4a31b2e990ae7bfabe67b4c58ca28666d9bcbce","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-b15ef876bf8ec2c0d0a9c37b75714988e1cd8d9e58c99a59c85c84cdd40f3e56-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Derive the repository key from one shared helper used by both `cli::batch_review::enable` and `run.rs`'s `build_batch_reviewer` call site, and add a test that runs `enable` for a repository and then asserts `resolved_tier_policy` returns that scoped policy for the key the daemon actually computes for the same repository.","status":"open","supersedes":null}]}
Found the two arms exactly as described (lines 741 and 748). Fixing both to `continue`.
Now let me verify the fix compiles and passes the relevant test suite.
Compiles cleanly (the pre-existing dead-code warning is unrelated). The fix is minimal and matches the required remediation exactly.

**Summary:** Changed the two `return;` arms (client construction failure, credential resolution failure) inside `poll_once`'s `for row in pending` loop in `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs` to `continue;`, matching the existing `batch_auth` arm's behavior. This prevents one repository's misconfigured credentials from starving polling (and deadline-expiry checks) for all other pending batches. No other files touched; no commit made.
