{"review_id":"00001788454764715784-0001485283-000001-cycle-review-2","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:a4f5dcdd245ca05269a0f59c0d44466ec9d515f2188b15262956bf96bf3c3266","findings":[{"finding_id":"f1-poll-aborts-all-rows","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"Per-row poll failures no longer abort the whole poll pass","claim":"Resolved. Both previously-offending arms in `poll_once` — `AnthropicHttpClient::new` failure and `EnvCredentialResolver.resolve` failure — now `continue` to the next pending row instead of `return`ing out of the function, matching the adjacent `batch_auth` arm. A single unresolvable row no longer starves every other pending batch of polling.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,919 @@\n+        let client = match AnthropicHttpClient::new(AnthropicHttpConfig::default()) {\n+            Ok(client) => client,\n+            Err(error) => {\n+                tracing::warn!(error = %error, \"batch review poll: client construction failed\");\n+                continue;\n+            }\n+        };\n+        let api_key = match EnvCredentialResolver.resolve(&auth) {\n+            Ok(key) => key,\n+            Err(error) => {\n+                tracing::warn!(error = %error, \"batch review poll: credential resolution failed\");\n+                continue;\n+            }\n+        };"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:ee66d22dae19901622351cbf121210e0244e6ae9184689aea8216752047a658e","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73382,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:a6634e80ef598af5a4bd51c0d1170262e640420e3043ebf3a6d1f9e143cb2ae2","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-ee66d22dae19901622351cbf121210e0244e6ae9184689aea8216752047a658e-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"No further action; the `continue` arms are in place.","status":"resolved","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f2-expiry-skipped-when-auth-unresolvable","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"Bounded-wait expiry is never evaluated for rows whose auth or credential cannot be resolved","claim":"In `poll_once` the deadline comparison (`Utc::now() >= deadline` -> `mark_expired_fallback`) is computed only *after* `batch_auth`, `AnthropicHttpClient::new`, and `EnvCredentialResolver.resolve` all succeed; each of those failure arms `continue`s before the deadline is ever read. `batch_auth` returns `None` whenever the repository's effective `batch_risk_classes` is empty, so running `familiar-ai batch-review disable` on the last configured class (or removing the auth profile, or unsetting the credential env var) orphans every already-submitted row for that repository: it is never polled, never expires, and stays `state='submitted'` forever while its PRD remains parked in review-pending. That defeats acceptance criterion 5 ('on expiry the review falls back to the interactive tier ... the PRD never sits in review-pending past the bound'), because the bound is only enforced on the paths that require live provider access. The tests cover expiry only through `BatchReviewAgent::poll_or_expire` (`expiry_falls_back_to_interactive_review_with_a_durable_reason`), which checks the deadline before resolving a credential; no test exercises the daemon poller's ordering.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,919 @@\n+    for row in pending {\n+        let Some(auth) = batch_auth(config, &row.repository_key) else {\n+            tracing::warn!(\n+                review_id = %row.review_id,\n+                repository_key = %row.repository_key,\n+                \"batch review poll: repository batch policy is no longer configured\"\n+            );\n+            continue;\n+        };\n+        let client = match AnthropicHttpClient::new(AnthropicHttpConfig::default()) {\n+            Ok(client) => client,\n+            Err(error) => {\n+                tracing::warn!(error = %error, \"batch review poll: client construction failed\");\n+                continue;\n+            }\n+        };\n+        let api_key = match EnvCredentialResolver.resolve(&auth) {\n+            Ok(key) => key,\n+            Err(error) => {\n+                tracing::warn!(error = %error, \"batch review poll: credential resolution failed\");\n+                continue;\n+            }\n+        };\n+        let deadline = chrono::DateTime::parse_from_rfc3339(&row.deadline_at)\n+            .map(|value| value.with_timezone(&Utc))\n+            .unwrap_or_else(|_| Utc::now());\n+        let outcome = if Utc::now() >= deadline {"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:ee66d22dae19901622351cbf121210e0244e6ae9184689aea8216752047a658e","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73382,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:a6634e80ef598af5a4bd51c0d1170262e640420e3043ebf3a6d1f9e143cb2ae2","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-ee66d22dae19901622351cbf121210e0244e6ae9184689aea8216752047a658e-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Evaluate the deadline before any auth, client, or credential resolution in `poll_once`: read `row.deadline_at` first and, when it has passed, call `mark_expired_fallback` and `resume_now` unconditionally (an expiry needs no provider access). Keep the resolution failures as `continue` only on the not-yet-expired path, and add a poller-level test that a row whose repository policy has since been disabled still transitions to `expired_fallback` within its bound.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f3-enable-snapshots-global-tier-policy","category":"maintainability_issue","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"`batch-review enable` freezes a copy of the global tier policy into the repository, decoupling it from future global changes","claim":"`tier_policy_table` seeds a newly created `repositories.<key>.review.tier_policy` from `seed_tier_policy_item(effective_policy)`, which serializes the whole effective (usually global) `ReviewTierPolicyConfig` — `independent_review_required`, `full_review_risk_classes`, and every `rules` entry — into the repository table. Because `resolved_tier_policy` prefers the repository-scoped policy exclusively (`.or(config.review.tier_policy.as_ref())` only fires when the scoped one is absent), that copy is a permanent snapshot: after a single `batch-review enable`, a later tightening of the global `[review.tier_policy]` — e.g. adding a risk class to `full_review_risk_classes` — silently stops applying to that repository. The seeding solves the opposite hazard (fields reverting to type defaults) but converts inheritance into a one-time fork, which is exactly the class of silent divergence acceptance criterion 6 is guarding against.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/cli/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,372 @@\n+fn tier_policy_table<'a>(\n+    document: &'a mut Document,\n+    repository_key: &str,\n+    effective_policy: &ReviewTierPolicyConfig,\n+) -> Result<&'a mut Table, String> {\n+    let repositories = root_table(document, \"repositories\")?;\n+    if !repositories.contains_key(repository_key) {\n+        repositories.insert(repository_key, Item::Table(Table::new()));\n+    }\n+    let repository = repositories\n+        .get_mut(repository_key)\n+        .and_then(Item::as_table_mut)\n+        .ok_or(\"repository config is not a table\")?;\n+    if !repository.contains_key(\"review\") {\n+        repository.insert(\"review\", Item::Table(Table::new()));\n+    }\n+    let review = repository\n+        .get_mut(\"review\")\n+        .and_then(Item::as_table_mut)\n+        .ok_or(\"review config is not a table\")?;\n+    if !review.contains_key(\"tier_policy\") {\n+        review.insert(\"tier_policy\", seed_tier_policy_item(effective_policy)?);\n+    }"},{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,919 @@\n+pub(crate) fn resolved_tier_policy<'a>(\n+    config: &'a Config,\n+    repository_key: &str,\n+) -> Option<&'a familiar_ai_core::config::ReviewTierPolicyConfig> {\n+    config\n+        .repositories\n+        .get(repository_key)\n+        .and_then(|repository| repository.review.as_ref())\n+        .and_then(|review| review.tier_policy.as_ref())\n+        .or(config.review.tier_policy.as_ref())\n+}"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:ee66d22dae19901622351cbf121210e0244e6ae9184689aea8216752047a658e","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73382,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:a6634e80ef598af5a4bd51c0d1170262e640420e3043ebf3a6d1f9e143cb2ae2","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-ee66d22dae19901622351cbf121210e0244e6ae9184689aea8216752047a658e-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Write only the batch-specific keys (`batch_risk_classes`, `max_batch_wait_ms`, `batch_worker`) into the repository-scoped table and have `resolved_tier_policy` merge a repository override onto the global policy field-by-field (repository value where present, global value otherwise), so non-batch fields keep inheriting. If a whole-policy snapshot is genuinely intended, state that in the CLI output and document that the repository stops tracking global tier-policy changes, and add a test asserting that a subsequent global `full_review_risk_classes` addition still reaches an enabled repository.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f4-batch-cost-is-an-assumed-rate","category":"unverifiable_claim","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Batch cost is a hardcoded 1/2 assumption, not a provider-reported figure","claim":"Acceptance criterion 4 requires each batch execution's accounting observation to record 'the provider-reported cost with provider provenance, so PRD-051 comparisons measure the actual discount ... rather than assuming a rate'. The implementation records `provider_cost_lexical: None` on the observation and derives the amount from `batch_cost_estimate`, which divides the configured *interactive* rate by the compile-time constant `BATCH_PRICE_DISCOUNT_DENOMINATOR: u64 = 2`. The recorded figure is therefore an assumed rate by construction, and the only test that exercises it (`batch_completion_records_a_batch_discounted_cost_distinct_from_interactive_rate`) asserts precisely `recorded_microusd == interactive_amount / 2`, i.e. it verifies the assumption rather than any provider-reported cost. The code comments are honest about this and the `rates_json` tags provenance as `batch-rate-estimate`, so nothing is misrepresented in the ledger — but the criterion's stated purpose (measuring the actual discount) cannot be verified from this change, since any PRD-051 comparison of batch against interactive for the same tokens will return exactly 2x by definition.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,919 @@\n+const BATCH_PRICE_DISCOUNT_DENOMINATOR: u64 = 2;\n+\n+fn batch_cost_estimate(\n+    pricing: &BTreeMap<String, familiar_ai_core::ExecutionPrice>,\n+    model: Option<&str>,\n+    input_tokens: Option<u64>,\n+    output_tokens: Option<u64>,\n+) -> Option<(u64, String, String)> {\n+    let model = model?;\n+    let price = pricing.get(model)?;\n+    let (interactive_cost, _, _) =\n+        crate::run::calculate_cost(input_tokens, Some(0), output_tokens, Some(price));\n+    let interactive_amount = interactive_cost?;\n+    let batch_amount = interactive_amount / BATCH_PRICE_DISCOUNT_DENOMINATOR;"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:ee66d22dae19901622351cbf121210e0244e6ae9184689aea8216752047a658e","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73382,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:a6634e80ef598af5a4bd51c0d1170262e640420e3043ebf3a6d1f9e143cb2ae2","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-ee66d22dae19901622351cbf121210e0244e6ae9184689aea8216752047a658e-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Either wire the batch observation into the same provider billing reconciliation PRD-051 already uses for real provider-reported costs (leaving `provider_cost_lexical` populated once reconciled) and treat the /2 figure strictly as a placeholder estimate, or make the discount factor a configured per-model rate (a `batch_*_microusd_per_million` alongside the interactive rates) so an operator can correct it without a code change. In either case, document that ledger comparisons of the estimate path cannot measure an actual discount, so PRD-051 does not report a tautological 2x.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f5-repository-key-round-trip-untested","category":"test_gap","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"No test proves the CLI-written `repositories.<key>` matches the key the daemon looks up","claim":"Batch tiering activates only if the map key `familiar-ai batch-review enable` writes under `repositories.<key>` is byte-identical to the key `build_batch_reviewer` looks up. The CLI derives it from `repository_identity(&current)` (canonicalized path — its own unit test builds the key by creating a plain `root/repo` directory and canonicalizing it), while `run_review` derives it from `slash(&context.repository.repository)`. `resolved_tier_policy`'s doc comment asserts these are 'exactly the map key the CLI writes', but no test exercises the round trip: `build_batch_reviewer_resolves_a_repository_scoped_enablement` inserts and looks up the same literal `\"repo/.git\"` (a git-dir shape, unlike the CLI test's plain-directory shape), so it proves the lookup logic but not the key derivation agreement. If the two derivations differ, `enable` silently succeeds while every review continues on the interactive tier — a failure that produces no error and would not be caught by any check in this plan.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/run.rs","hunk":"@@ -2437,6 +2456,16 @@ fn run_review(input: ReviewRunInput<'_>) -> Result<ReviewCycle, RunError> {\n             path_prefixes: check.path_prefixes.clone(),\n         })\n         .collect();\n+    let batch_reviewer = crate::batch_review::build_batch_reviewer(\n+        config,\n+        db.conn(),\n+        format!(\"{execution_id}-cycle\"),\n+        slash(&context.repository.repository),\n+        prd_id.into(),\n+        execution_id.into(),\n+        declared_risk_classes.clone(),\n+        &reviewer_adapter,\n+    );\n     let coordinator = ReviewCoordinator {\n         collector: &collector,\n         verifier: &verifier,"},{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/cli/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,372 @@\n+    fn repository_key(root: &std::path::Path) -> String {\n+        let repository = root.join(\"repo\");\n+        std::fs::create_dir_all(&repository).unwrap();\n+        repository\n+            .canonicalize()\n+            .unwrap()\n+            .to_string_lossy()\n+            .into_owned()\n+    }"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:ee66d22dae19901622351cbf121210e0244e6ae9184689aea8216752047a658e","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73382,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:a6634e80ef598af5a4bd51c0d1170262e640420e3043ebf3a6d1f9e143cb2ae2","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-ee66d22dae19901622351cbf121210e0244e6ae9184689aea8216752047a658e-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Add a test that derives the key through the CLI's `repository_identity` for a real fixture worktree, writes the enablement with that key, then resolves it through the same derivation `run_review` uses (`slash(&context.repository.repository)`) and asserts `build_batch_reviewer` returns `Some`. Alternatively, factor a single `repository_key(...)` helper used by both the CLI and `run_review` so the two derivations cannot drift.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f6-workspace-tests-failing-advisory","category":"unverifiable_claim","severity":"low","blocking":false,"title":"Workspace-wide test run fails; the change's effect outside the five named crates is unverified","claim":"The `tests-workspace-advisory` check (`cargo test --workspace --no-default-features --no-fail-fast`) exited 101 with status `failed`. It is marked `required: false`, and the required `tests-green-crates` check covers only `familiar-ai-core`, `familiar-ai-review`, `familiar-ai-agent`, `familiar-ai-storage`, and `familiar-ai-daemon`. This change also modifies `familiar-ai-llm` (`anthropic_api.rs`, ~200 new lines plus four new tests), which is outside the required check's package list, and adds a public variant to `ReviewTier` plus a new field to `ReviewCycle` that other workspace crates may consume. Because the advisory run's stdout/stderr are referenced only by content hash and not disclosed in this package, I cannot determine whether its failures pre-date this change or were introduced by it, and therefore cannot confirm the change is green across the workspace.","evidence":[{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-workspace-advisory","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:ef7b1c36ecf75b8dbad0e515fd7992aa16a6a7f089fb68743bde1a64680f0643","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":100919,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:a6634e80ef598af5a4bd51c0d1170262e640420e3043ebf3a6d1f9e143cb2ae2","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-ef7b1c36ecf75b8dbad0e515fd7992aa16a6a7f089fb68743bde1a64680f0643-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Either add `familiar-ai-llm` to the required `tests-green-crates` package list (this change adds substantive code and tests there), or disclose the advisory run's failing test names so the pre-existing failures can be distinguished from any regression introduced here.","status":"open","supersedes":null}]}
