{"review_id":"00001788454764715784-0001485283-000001-cycle-review-3","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:6653c995311edfc622c4b42c84f11f4a4ccf957dd61a664fd22170a670bd7e8f","findings":[{"finding_id":"f2-expiry-skipped-when-auth-unresolvable","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"Poller now evaluates the bounded-wait deadline before any provider access","claim":"Resolved. `poll_once` now reads `row.deadline_at` and performs the `mark_expired_fallback` transition at the top of the loop body, before `batch_auth`, `AnthropicHttpClient::new`, and `EnvCredentialResolver.resolve` are attempted, with an explanatory comment; each of those resolution failures now `continue`s only after the deadline has already been enforced. A new integration test (`poller_expires_a_row_whose_repository_policy_is_no_longer_configured`) drives `poll_once` with `Config::default()` (so `batch_auth` returns `None`) against a row whose deadline is in the past and asserts the row reaches `expired_fallback` with reason `max_batch_wait_exceeded`.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,939 @@\n+    for row in pending {\n+        // The deadline is evaluated before any auth, client, or credential\n+        // resolution: expiry needs no provider access, so a repository whose\n+        // batch policy, worker, or credential has since become unresolvable\n+        // must still fall back to interactive review within its bound\n+        // instead of being silently orphaned in `submitted` forever.\n+        let deadline = chrono::DateTime::parse_from_rfc3339(&row.deadline_at)\n+            .map(|value| value.with_timezone(&Utc))\n+            .unwrap_or_else(|_| Utc::now());\n+        if Utc::now() >= deadline {"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:a0b2b7503de7849fadacf8b646639dad2ce83447742ad952e71826256ee15341","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73465,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:4d321d9f601c09ea6930b8945a1008c40ac386cbee1b7ad02359ff3a369104c2","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-a0b2b7503de7849fadacf8b646639dad2ce83447742ad952e71826256ee15341-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"No further action; the ordering defect and its missing regression coverage are both addressed.","status":"resolved","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f3-completed-rows-never-repolled","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"The daemon poller never revisits a `completed` batch row, so a failed or crashed resume strands the result","claim":"`poll_once` builds its work set exclusively from `BatchReviewRepository::submitted()`, whose SQL is `WHERE state='submitted'`. Once a poll pass calls `apply_batch_result` -> `mark_completed`, the row leaves that set permanently, and the only thing that consumes it is the single fire-and-forget `resume_now` call made immediately afterwards. Every failure arm inside `resume_now` (`Database::open` failure, `CheckpointRepository::get` returning `Ok(None)` or `Err`, `resolved_agent_entries` failure, `resolved_remediation_entry` failure) simply logs and returns, and a daemon crash between the `mark_completed` write and the spawned blocking task doing its work has the same effect. The doc comment on `resume_now` states 'the next poll (or an operator-triggered resume) tries again from the same durable state', but the next poll provably cannot: it never selects `completed` rows. The `BatchReviewAgent`'s own bounded-wait expiry also does not apply, because `poll_or_expire` is reached only from `BatchReviewState::Submitted`. The result is a durable row holding a paid-for, unapplied review result that the daemon-owned lifecycle will not re-drive, contrary to acceptance criterion 3 ('a crash between submission and completion resumes polling from durable state, never loses a submitted batch'), leaving recovery to whatever unrelated resume happens to touch the PRD next.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,939 @@\n+pub async fn poll_once(db: &Arc<Mutex<Database>>, config: &Config, paths: &AppPaths) {\n+    let pending = {\n+        let guard = db.lock().unwrap();\n+        BatchReviewRepository::new(guard.conn()).submitted()\n+    };"},{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-storage/src/repos/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,412 @@\n+    /// All rows still awaiting a provider result, across every repository —\n+    /// the daemon's poll set, and the source of \"resume polling from durable\n+    /// state\" after a crash or restart.\n+    pub fn submitted(&self) -> familiar_ai_core::Result<Vec<BatchReviewRow>> {\n+        let mut statement = self\n+            .conn\n+            .prepare(&format!(\n+                \"{SELECT_ROW} WHERE state='submitted' ORDER BY submitted_at\"\n+            ))\n+            .map_err(db)?;"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:a0b2b7503de7849fadacf8b646639dad2ce83447742ad952e71826256ee15341","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73465,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:4d321d9f601c09ea6930b8945a1008c40ac386cbee1b7ad02359ff3a369104c2","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-a0b2b7503de7849fadacf8b646639dad2ce83447742ad952e71826256ee15341-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Make the poller's work set include unapplied `completed` rows as well as `submitted` ones (e.g. a `pending()` query over `state IN ('submitted','completed')`, dispatching completed rows straight to `resume_now`), so a resume that fails or is interrupted is retried on the next tick. Add a regression test that marks a row `completed`, runs `poll_once`, and asserts the row is re-driven; and correct the `resume_now` doc comment, which currently asserts a retry the code does not perform.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f4-batch-cost-is-an-assumed-discount","category":"unverifiable_claim","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Batch ledger cost is a locally assumed 50% discount, not a provider-reported cost with provider provenance","claim":"Acceptance criterion 4 requires each batch execution's accounting observation to record 'the provider-reported cost with provider provenance, so PRD-051 comparisons measure the actual discount ... rather than assuming a rate'. The implementation records the opposite: `record_accounting` sets `provider_cost_lexical: None` and `provider_request_id: None`, and the only monetary figure written is `batch_cost_estimate`'s output — the configured interactive rate divided by the hard-coded constant `BATCH_PRICE_DISCOUNT_DENOMINATOR = 2`, tagged `\"provenance\": \"batch-rate-estimate\"` and appended through `append_legacy_configured_estimate`. The discount is therefore assumed, not measured, and the regression test asserts exactly that assumption (`assert_eq!(recorded_microusd, interactive_amount / 2)`), so a PRD-051 comparison of batch against interactive reviews of the same risk class will recover the constant 2 by construction rather than an observed rate. The in-code justification ('Anthropic's Message Batches API is documented to bill at half the interactive per-token rate') is a claim about provider behaviour that nothing in the change or the verification evidence substantiates, and it is not reconciled against any provider-reported billing figure. The batch tier is still distinguishable in the ledger (`service_tier = \"batch\"`, `adapter = \"anthropic-api\"`), which is the partitioning half of the criterion, but the cost half is an estimate presented where a provider fact was required.","evidence":[{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,939 @@\n+const BATCH_PRICE_DISCOUNT_DENOMINATOR: u64 = 2;\n+\n+fn batch_cost_estimate(\n+    pricing: &BTreeMap<String, familiar_ai_core::ExecutionPrice>,\n+    model: Option<&str>,\n+    input_tokens: Option<u64>,\n+    output_tokens: Option<u64>,\n+) -> Option<(u64, String, String)> {\n+    let model = model?;\n+    let price = pricing.get(model)?;\n+    let (interactive_cost, _, _) =\n+        crate::run::calculate_cost(input_tokens, Some(0), output_tokens, Some(price));\n+    let interactive_amount = interactive_cost?;\n+    let batch_amount = interactive_amount / BATCH_PRICE_DISCOUNT_DENOMINATOR;"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:a0b2b7503de7849fadacf8b646639dad2ce83447742ad952e71826256ee15341","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":73465,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:4d321d9f601c09ea6930b8945a1008c40ac386cbee1b7ad02359ff3a369104c2","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-a0b2b7503de7849fadacf8b646639dad2ce83447742ad952e71826256ee15341-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Either (a) record the provider's own reported figure where one exists — carry the batch member's `provider_request_id` and reconcile the observation against the provider billing import PRD-051 already ingests, leaving `provider_cost_lexical` populated from that source — or (b) if no provider-reported cost is obtainable for a batch member, state that explicitly in the PRD's acceptance evidence and have the ledger mark the observation as rate-assumed so downstream comparisons cannot mistake the derived figure for a measured discount. In either case, replace the hard-coded `BATCH_PRICE_DISCOUNT_DENOMINATOR` with a configured, auditable rate rather than a compile-time constant.","status":"open","supersedes":null}]}
